\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Covering sums of the increment metric}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Let #{d} be the ultrametric of \ref{fdip-00FI}, and set
##{\alpha=\frac{2\log p}{\log(1/(p\lambda))}.}
For a countable cover of #{X} by nonempty sets, form the sum
of the #{s}-powers of their #{d}-diameters. For #{s=0}, take
each power to be #{1}. For every #{s>\alpha} the infimum of
these sums is #{0}. For every #{0\le s\le\alpha} the infimum
is positive. The infimum of the orders #{s} for which the
covering infimum vanishes therefore equals #{\alpha}.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{C} be the positive prefactor in \ref{fdip-00FI}, and
set #{r_*=p\lambda} and #{\delta_n=C^{1/2}r_*^{n/2}}. A
depth-#{n} cylinder has diameter #{\delta_n}: any two of its
points meet at depth at least #{n}, and it contains two
points that meet at depth exactly #{n}. The space #{X} is the
union of the #{p^n} cylinders of depth #{n}. Their
#{s}-power sum equals
##{p^n\delta_n^s=C^{s/2}\ue^{n\log p+(ns/2)\log r_*}.}
The coefficient of #{n} is negative precisely when
#{s>\alpha}, because
#{\frac{\alpha}{2}=\log p/\log(1/r_*)}. The sums tend to #{0}, so the
infimum vanishes for every #{s>\alpha}.}

\p{The same computation with #{s=\alpha} uses
#{r_*^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}=p^{-1}}, hence
#{\delta_n^{\alpha}=C^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}p^{-n}}. Suppose
#{0<\operatorname{diam}(U)<\delta_n}. Any two points of #{U}
satisfy #{d<\delta_n}, so their meeting depth is at least
#{n+1} and #{U} lies in one cylinder of depth #{n+1}. If
#{\delta_{n+1}\le\operatorname{diam}(U)<\delta_n}, let #{B}
be a depth-#{(n+1)} cylinder containing #{U}. Then
#{\eta(B)=p^{-(n+1)}} and
#{\delta_{n+1}^{\alpha}=C^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}p^{-(n+1)}}, so
##{\eta(B)\le C^{-\frac{\alpha}{2}}\operatorname{diam}(U)^{\alpha}.}
A set of diameter #{0} is a singleton. It lies in a
depth-#{k} cylinder of measure #{p^{-k}} for every #{k}, so
its measure is #{0}. Countable additivity gives measure #{0}
to every countable set of singletons.}

\p{Take #{s=\alpha} and a countable cover. Singletons
contribute nothing. A set of diameter at least #{\delta_0}
contributes at least #{\delta_0^{\alpha}}. If every positive
diameter is strictly less than #{\delta_0}, each such set
#{U} lies in a cylinder #{B} to which the preceding bound
applies. That bound rearranges to
#{\operatorname{diam}(U)^{\alpha}\ge C^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}\eta(B)}.
Those cylinders cover a set of measure #{1}, so the sum of
their measures is at least #{1} and the sum of the
#{\alpha}-powers is at least #{C^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}}.}

\p{Now take #{0<s<\alpha}. If some set in the cover has
diameter at least #{\delta_0}, that one term is at least
#{\delta_0^s}. Otherwise every diameter is strictly less
than #{\delta_0}. A singleton contributes #{0}. Each
remaining set #{U} satisfies
#{0<\operatorname{diam}(U)<\delta_0}, hence
#{\operatorname{diam}(U)^{s-\alpha}\ge\delta_0^{s-\alpha}}
since #{s-\alpha<0}. The bound above gives a cylinder
#{C_U} containing #{U} with
#{\eta(C_U)\le C^{-\frac{\alpha}{2}}\operatorname{diam}(U)^{\alpha}},
and therefore
##{\operatorname{diam}(U)^s \ge\delta_0^{s-\alpha}C^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}\eta(C_U).}
In this remaining case the cylinders cover every point
outside the singletons. Those singletons form a set of
measure #{0}, so the sum of the cylinder measures is at
least #{1}. Since #{\delta_0^{\alpha}=C^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}},
the constant #{\delta_0^{s-\alpha}C^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}} equals
#{\delta_0^s}. The small-diameter covering sum is therefore
at least #{\delta_0^s}, the same bound as the one term from
a set of diameter at least #{\delta_0}. For #{s=0} the
content is the least number of
nonempty sets in a cover, hence equals #{1}.}
}
